+## The portal (read-only pages)
+
+`/ui/` shows the lead book in a browser: a summary that adds up, the leads, and the items that need a person. The pages read the lead book through a second, read-only database connection (a write through it fails, and one test shows that); the buttons that retry or resolve an item call the same `/review` routes as everything else. Every request, including the pages, must come with a `Host` header that names this computer (or a name from `DESK_ALLOWED_HOSTS`), and a request from a page on another address (a different `Origin`, or `Sec-Fetch-Site` other than `same-origin`) is refused with 403 before anything is read. The exact routes are in `contract.md`.
+
+**What the Host and Origin check does and does not do.** It stops web pages in a browser: a page on another web site, and a web site that tricks the browser into talking to this computer under another name ("DNS rebinding"). It does NOT stop a person or program that can reach the desk's address and sends the right `Host` header by hand. There is no login. So keep the desk on `127.0.0.1` (the default). If a container (n8n in Docker, for example) must reach it, bind to the one address that only your container network reaches (the Docker bridge or host address), never to the wildcard address (the one made of four zeros). A bind to any other address than this computer's own exposes every lead (names, e-mail addresses, phone numbers, messages) to everyone who can connect, and the desk prints a warning at start-up when you do that. `/api/ui` answers at most 8 requests at once; more get 503 `busy`. The count "refused at the door" on the Overview page is kept in memory and starts again at 0 when the desk restarts: read it as "since desk start (HH:MM)". The desk serves each connection on its own thread and sets no limit on the number of threads: a client that holds many connections open (slow bytes) holds that many threads, so keep the desk off networks you do not trust. A bind written as a short all-zeros form (for example the single digit 0) is treated as a wildcard. Close the portal while you run a load test: every open page asks the desk for its numbers every few seconds.
+
