 ## Crash hook ownership
 `lead_desk.hooks.FlagFileHooks(flag_path)` is the single shared hook for every subprocess crash. It reads a one-shot flag file (the path comes from config or the launcher, content = a point name), deletes it, prints "simulate crash" and calls `os._exit(137)`. `tests/crash_launcher.py` and `scripts/video_crash.py` contain no hook code: each only calls `lead_desk.server.main(hooks=FlagFileHooks(path))`. In-process tests use their own `Hooks` subclass that raises `SimulatedCrash`. Hook A fires only when this call created the receipt row; hook B fires only when this call committed transaction 2 (a replay never fires either).
 
+## Host and Origin gate
+Every request, on every route, passes `_gate_error` in `server.py` before the path is read. (1) `Host` is required (sent once) and its name, port ignored and lower-cased, must be `127.0.0.1`, `localhost`, `[::1]`, the bind address (not when it is a wildcard address), or a name in `Config.allowed_hosts` (`DESK_ALLOWED_HOSTS`, comma list, each name a lower-case host name made of dot-separated labels of 1 to 63 letters, digits or hyphens that neither start nor end with a hyphen, at most 253 characters in all, or an IPv6 address in brackets; a wildcard address (four zeros, or `::`) is refused). A `Host` port above 65535 is refused too. Otherwise 403 `{"error":"forbidden_host"}`. (2) An `Origin` header, if present (sent once), must equal `http://<allowed name>:<bound port>` exactly; `null` and anything else give 403 `{"error":"forbidden_origin"}`. (3) A `Sec-Fetch-Site` header, if present, must be `same-origin` or `none`, else 403 `{"error":"forbidden_origin"}`. A refused request writes nothing: no receipt, no count in `rejected_envelopes`; its body is read and dropped before the reply (the desk answers as HTTP/1.0 and never sends `100 Continue`; for a request with `Expect: 100-continue` the body is not waited for, so the refusal is immediate). Requests without `Origin` and `Sec-Fetch-Site` (n8n's server side, scripts, tests) pass on `Host` alone. **The gate protects against browsers only** (cross-site pages and DNS rebinding). It is not authentication: anyone who can reach the bound address and types the right `Host` reads every lead. Default bind is loopback; for a container bind the one bridge or host address, never a wildcard; the desk prints a warning at start-up when the bind is not loopback. A caller that reaches the desk under another name (for example n8n in Docker, which uses the host name Docker gives the computer) sets `DESK_BIND` to the address the desk must listen on and `DESK_ALLOWED_HOSTS` to that name.
+
+## Portal routes
+Read-only pages for a person: `GET /ui` answers 308 to `/ui/`; `GET /ui/<file>` serves one of the `.html`, `.css`, `.js` files found under `src/lead_desk/portal/` when the server started (a fixed map, never a path read from the request; anything else is 404); `/ui/demo/<file>` serves a file of the demo extension when one is installed, else 404. `GET /api/ui/summary`, `/api/ui/leads`, `/api/ui/leads/<lead_id>` and `/api/ui/review` answer JSON built from ONE read transaction on a separate read-only connection (`mode=ro`, `PRAGMA query_only=ON`): a write through it fails. All four, `/api/ui/review` included, read through that connection only (the review list runs `desk.review_rows`, the desk's own SQL and hint rules, on it); the desk's write connections are never used for a page. At most `PORTAL_MAX_INFLIGHT` (8) of these requests run at once; more get 503 `{"error":"busy"}`. `summary.refused_at_door` counts requests refused at the door since the desk started (in memory, 0 again after a restart); show it as "since desk start (HH:MM)". `GET /api/ui/leads?q=` matches (as plain text; case is ignored for ASCII letters only) the name, email, company, lead id and first request id, at most 100 characters, no control characters. Files are read once, when `portal_api` is first imported; symbolic links, files that resolve outside the portal folder and files over `STATIC_FILE_MAX_BYTES` are left out, and the folder name `demo/` is reserved for the extension: a file of the portal folder under that name is never served. Any other method on these routes is 405. `/api/demo/<rest>` goes to the demo extension (GET and POST only, any other method is 405; 404 when none is installed or when the desk does not run the demo adapters; a POST body must be `application/json`, else 400 `{"error":"bad_body"}`; a body over `DEMO_BODY_MAX` bytes is 413 `{"error":"too_large"}` and is not handed on; a body that stalls is 408 `{"error":"request_timeout"}`). The extension is loaded before the socket is bound, so a broken extension leaves nothing listening. The "Follow-up failed" card says "nothing was sent" only for the reasons `refused`, `unreachable` and `internal:*`; the reasons a person's own choice leaves (`person: not sent`: after the email question, "Follow-up failed; a person marked the email as not sent", Retry sends it now, with a reminder to check the mail system first; after the CRM question (the row's `crm_done` is 0 again), "Follow-up failed; a person marked the CRM row as missing", Retry asks the CRM again and then sends the email; `person: crm row present`: "Follow-up not finished; the CRM row is confirmed", Retry goes straight to the email) have their own words and never say the email may have left (the reminder to check the mail system is the only hedge); any other reason (`ambiguous`, `crash_while_sending`, others), and any other failed follow-up whose row carries `ambiguous_prior` (an earlier send may have left), shows "Follow-up did not finish", because the email may have left, and Retry is safe only on a keyed outbox. Every reply, JSON or file, carries `Content-Security-Policy` (no inline script or style, no `data:`), `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`, `Referrer-Policy: no-referrer`, `Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin` and `Cache-Control: no-store`.
